  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><msqrt><mi>x</mi></msqrt></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. It is strictly increasing, starts at zero and takes every nonnegative output, so it maps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x0221E;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> onto <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x0221E;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Solving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>, since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Thus its increasing inverse is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> The given fact reflects these graphs in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>. Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, their two areas over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> fill the rectangle with side lengths 4 and 3. Therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x0222B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> The requested integrand is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Reflection in the vertical midpoint line <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> sends the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to itself and preserves its area.</p><p><strong>4.</strong> The requested integral is consequently <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, answer E. All logarithm arguments on the relevant interval are positive. This uses graph geometry, not a logarithmic or exponential antiderivative.</p>"
}
